::CW_U06_L07_GP_Q01::[MLA.CW.PUB.01] Which portfolio choice is strongest?{
~A. Choose the longest piece only because it fills space.#Incorrect. Length alone does not prove portfolio value.
~B. Choose the favorite piece with no explanation.#Incorrect. A portfolio choice needs evidence and rationale.
~C. Choose a piece with unresolved draft comments.#Incorrect. Final portfolio work should be reader-ready.
=D. Choose a revised piece that shows clear craft growth and can be explained.#Correct. This connects selection, revision, and author reasoning.
}

::CW_U06_L07_GP_Q02::[MLA.CW.COM.01] A student notices the author statement discusses imagery, but no selected piece shows imagery. What should the student do?{
~A. Leave the mismatch because readers will guess.#Incorrect. Readers should not have to guess the connection.
~B. Remove all craft language from the statement.#Incorrect. Craft language is needed for author reasoning.
=C. Revise the statement or portfolio so the evidence and explanation match.#Correct. The statement and portfolio should support each other.
~D. Add random images to every page.#Incorrect. Random additions do not create purposeful evidence.
}

::CW_U06_L07_GP_Q03::[MLA.CW.PUB.01] Which portfolio order explanation is strongest?{
=A. I placed the story first because it shows revised dialogue, then the poem because it extends voice through rhythm.#Correct. This explains a purposeful reader path. This answer matches the lesson skill and is supported by the specific creative-writing evidence in the prompt.
~B. I put the files in the order I found them.#Incorrect. Random file order does not guide readers.
~C. I did not think about order.#Incorrect. Portfolio order shapes the reader's experience.
~D. I used the shortest piece first because it was easiest.#Incorrect. Ease is not a strong reader purpose.
}

::CW_U06_L07_GP_Q04::[MLA.CW.COM.01] Which presentation plan best connects to the portfolio?{
~A. Read a random excerpt and give no context.#Incorrect. The audience needs a frame and focus.
=B. Select one excerpt, give brief context, name a craft focus, and explain revision growth.#Correct. This connects presentation to portfolio evidence. This answer matches the lesson skill and is supported by the specific creative-writing evidence in the prompt.
~C. Summarize every piece without discussing craft.#Incorrect. Summary alone does not explain author choices.
~D. Explain only why the writer likes the portfolio.#Incorrect. Personal preference is not enough. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U06_L07_GP_Q05::[MLA.CW.PUB.01] Which final readiness note is most useful?{
~A. Fix everything later.#Incorrect. This is too vague to guide action.
~B. It is probably fine.#Incorrect. This does not identify evidence of readiness.
~C. Add more decoration to make it look finished.#Incorrect. Decoration does not replace publication readiness. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=D. Remove draft comments from the poem and preview the file before submission.#Correct. This names specific reader-ready actions. This answer matches the lesson skill and is supported by the specific creative-writing evidence in the prompt.
}
